# `linprog::to_string`

The name of a linear-programming `Status` as text.

## Signature

```cpp
const char *linprog::to_string(linprog::Status value);
```

## What It Does

`Status` is carried in `Result::status` as a `Float64` code, so a lane's verdict prints as a number. `to_string` turns the code (cast back to `Status`) into its name: `Optimal`, `PrimalInfeasible`, `Unbounded`, `IterationLimit` or `NearOptimal`. Use it for reports and log lines. It returns a pointer to a static string.

## Parameters

`linprog::Status value`
: The status to name.

## Returns

A static, null-terminated name; `"Unknown"` for an invalid value.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

All five names.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  for (const linprog::Status status : {linprog::Status::Optimal, linprog::Status::PrimalInfeasible, linprog::Status::Unbounded, linprog::Status::IterationLimit, linprog::Status::NearOptimal}) {
    out.writeln(static_cast<int>(status), "->", linprog::to_string(status));
  }
}
```

### Example 2

Naming the verdicts of a batch. Two lanes of one call, one feasible and one infeasible, are reported by name.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <vector>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  linprog::Problem problem;
  problem.c = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 1.0}});
  problem.a_ub = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {-1.0, -1.0}});
  problem.b_ub = pluss::series::make({{4.0, -1.0}, {1.0, -3.0}});
  problem.lower = pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0});
  const linprog::Result result = linprog::solve(problem);
  const std::vector<double> codes = result.status.items<double>();
  for (std::size_t lane = 0; lane < codes.size(); ++lane) {
    out.writeln("lane", lane, ":", linprog::to_string(static_cast<linprog::Status>(static_cast<int>(codes[lane]))));
  }
}
```

### Example 3

The text of a code read from the result series of a single problem.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  linprog::Problem problem;
  problem.c = pluss::series::make({-1.0});
  problem.lower = pluss::series::make({0.0});
  const linprog::Result result = linprog::solve(problem);
  out.writeln("verdict =", linprog::to_string(static_cast<linprog::Status>(static_cast<int>(result.status.item<double>()))));
}
```

